 - Widgets consume display-only snapshots; network reads remain in the connected app and Android sync runs only with placed widgets. Unloaded stores and failed builders preserve native snapshots. Quota timestamps reflect provider answers, and failed refreshes cannot rewrite old data as fresh. Queued native refresh taps are acknowledged after fetching, including taps arriving during a fetch; bridge failure leaves them queued.
 - Widget session ordering, unread state and project ownership match the shared lifecycle and sidebar, including worktrees and isolated spaces. The iOS bridge returns a JSON primitive or null so failed construction cannot overwrite a good App Group snapshot. Widget quotas use the Usage page's selected providers, models, formatting and theme colors without copying credentials.
 
+## Mobile instance attention surfaces
+
+### Ownership
+
+`MobileInstanceAttentionBell` owns the phone's header bell and bottom sheet; `MobileInstanceAttentionBadge` owns the per-connection count in Instances. Both read `stores/useInstanceAttentionStore.ts` and open rows through `lib/instanceAttentionJump.ts`. `mobileConnections` owns saved connections and their removal.
+
+### Invariants
+
+- The bell uses the shared `MOBILE_HEADER_BUTTON_CLASS` size; the count sits inside that 40px control instead of widening it and shifting the header.
+- The connected server's waiting sessions are published under the reserved `openchamber:this-instance` id, so its badge reads that entry rather than the saved connection id.
+- A sheet opened for waiting sessions closes itself when the last one clears, instead of staying empty until the next item reopens it uninvited.
+- An unreachable instance is shown as the quiet note even when nothing else waits; a failed poll's last-known rows stay tappable only through a real connection.
+- Removing a saved connection drops its attention rows at once. A row whose connection is gone, or whose last poll failed, does not start a switch.
+- A password prompt or a failed connect keeps the sheet and its rows in place, so the tap is never a silent no-op.
+
 ## Decisions
 
 ### Why mobile push registers with every saved instance and not only the connected one
