@@ -100,7 +100,6 @@ The library owns shared session metadata readers, runtime transport and credenti
 - A forked session's canonical directory is the prompt's directory. Directory-child insertion normalizes Windows paths and remains necessary alongside the global metadata upsert.
 - A btw fork without its first question is unusable and is unlinked before deletion. Destruction unlinks first so a failed deletion cannot keep the parent's panel open; rename failure alone does not invalidate the fork.
 - Ordinary user forks drop source-owned links and scheduled-run markers, retain content metadata, and pause active goals so two sessions cannot pursue the same goal unattended. File-backed objectives are copied only while the fork still owns the same goal; repair failure leaves the already usable fork intact.
-- Forks map inherited context pins to copied messages when complete reads identify an unambiguous match. A temporary page-read failure gets one retry on the same runtime. If the read still fails, the pins stay untouched.
 - Work hints and assists describe only the latest idle turn and remain hidden during live work. Revert invalidates the assist, and a later idle timestamp retires stale hints even when a clearing event is missed.
 - Stored turn duration applies only to the idle transition it names. Metadata and session-record caches rely on immutable replacement; cached parsed data is shared read-only.
 - Display lineage follows same-folder work origins with cycle protection; plain forks and multi-run lanes remain roots. VS Code uses OpenCode parentage alone, and display lineage never changes server subsession semantics.
@@ -176,7 +175,6 @@ The library owns shared session metadata readers, runtime transport and credenti
 - Background network lanes share one fixed aggregate budget with reserved session-list capacity so slow Git and discovery reads cannot fill the browser pool and block interactive session opening. Retired work resolves as unavailable, never authoritative empty success.
 - Project action selections persist per runtime and project because the panel remounts. A deduplicated terminal response belongs to its creating peer, and directory listings use the same normalized keys as every terminal surface.
 - Terminal sockets stay open briefly through tab switches to avoid repeated auth, upgrades and replay. Frame decompression preserves arrival order, adjacent output joins before encoding to retain split surrogates, and accepted resizes update retained snapshot geometry. A failed gzip snapshot retries through a fresh attach without compression; pre-open auth failure discards the stale URL token.
-- A browser host terminal with no bearer, extra runtime headers, or relay first checks the resolver's same-origin `/auth/session`; a confirmed tunnel scope opens its same-origin cookie-backed socket without minting a URL token. Other runtimes and space terminals keep URL-token authentication.
 - Preview extraction preserves announced paths and offers every announced server because output chunk order cannot decide which app the user meant. Inferred commands require announcements; loose fallback candidates stay local. Portless names replace their underlying loopback port only where those names resolve on the correct machine; server probes remain loopback-only.
 - Project-action URL variables distinguish linked worktrees from the primary checkout. An unreadable linked branch fails instead of opening the main app, empty variables remove their separators, and truncated labels use a hash where secure-context crypto exists.
 - Code row heights stay on whole pixels across editor and diff views because fractional rows drift under virtualization. Mobile retains its pinned code sizing. Theme-derived agent colors allocate from the complete roster; sparse palettes reuse syntax roles, and legacy migrated `#aaaaaa` means no configured agent color.
@@ -220,3 +218,32 @@ Pairing and connection checks address a server before selecting it. Routing them
 ### Why a pairing link is cleaned around its edges and inside `p` only, and not across the whole text
 
 A link moved between machines picks up characters it never holds: line breaks from a wrapped mail or terminal line (a text field turns them into spaces), zero-width spaces and word joiners from chat apps, direction marks, a byte order mark. `parsePairingConnectionPayload` and `parsePairingConnectionPayloadString` drop whitespace, control and format characters around the text and from the decoded `p` value, which also covers the percent-encoded forms macOS hands over (`%0A`, `%E2%80%8B`). Removing them from the whole text was rejected: `& p=A&p=B` would become `&p=A&p=B`, and this parser would read A while the desktop shell, which confirms the server from the text as it came, showed B. The browser's `atob` refuses these characters where Node's `Buffer` skips them, so without the cleaning the shell's confirmation dialog accepts a link the window then rejects.
+
+## Cross-instance attention
+
+### Ownership
+
+`lib/instanceAttention.ts` owns the global wire reads (`/api/session`, `/api/sessions/status`, `/api/config/settings` and `/api/fs/home` for the folders a host opens, `/api/git/project-worktrees` for the worktrees outside them, and a session's own detail route for a request that fell outside the page), the ordering and subagent attribution, and the relay transport. `lib/attentionDirectories.ts` owns the folder rule both halves apply. `hooks/useInstanceAttentionPolling.ts` owns the desktop poller and its reachability decisions; `hooks/useLocalInstanceAttention.ts` publishes this window's own instance from the live stores. `stores/useInstanceAttentionStore.ts` holds the one sorted list every surface reads. `lib/instanceAttentionJump.ts` owns the shared switch-and-open path, and `lib/desktopHostActions.ts` owns the saved-host switch and the return to the app's own server.
+
+### Invariants
+
+- Both halves apply one folder rule, so the drawer cannot show a session the instance on screen hides. The local half filters with `isSessionAdmitted`; a polled half filters with the folders that host reports. A host that cannot answer (an unreadable settings or home route) leaves its rows standing rather than reading as "no folders", while a host that answers without any project is a real answer and admits only chats roots and spaces.
+- `lib/pendingFormVerification.ts` decides which indexed forms the server's own form list no longer holds: the host's cross-directory index keeps a form whose settlement event it missed (a question tool call that ended `error` never sent one), so the drawer asks OpenCode again for the directories of the forms it would show. A directory that could not be read keeps its forms, since absence from an unreadable answer proves nothing. The local half applies the plan and settles the index; a polled host's rows are filtered from the same answer inside the reader.
+- A pending form is checked against OpenCode's own form list before it becomes a row: the host's cross-directory index keeps a form whose settlement event it missed (a question tool call that ended `error` never sent one), so a polled host's rows are filtered by `/api/form` for that host, and the local half settles the index's stale forms from the same read when it mounts or the drawer is refreshed. A failed read keeps the form, since absence from an unreadable answer proves nothing.
+- The local half reads this window's own session list when the store holds none. A host switch clears that list, and the phone has no global poller to refill it, so without the read the drawer showed only the other instances' rows until the sessions sheet was opened.
+- The polled folders are the host's projects, the worktrees it lists under them, its chats roots, a `/spaces/<id>` folder and a deleted space's archived chats. A worktree outside every project path is the local half's normal case, so the poll reads `/api/git/project-worktrees` for it; when that read fails the project paths stand alone. A request whose session fell outside the 200-record page is resolved through its own detail route before the rule judges it, so a genuine request in a registered folder is kept and one in an unregistered folder is not shown.
+- The app's own server is a poll target (`local`) only while the window shows a saved host and its page can reach that server (`pageReachesLocalOrigin`: the packaged UI, or a page already on the local origin). A page another instance served is cross-origin to the local server, so it has to be reloaded into the Local UI before it can read this machine; reading it anyway would report Local as unreachable. The read goes straight to the shell's local origin with the local client token and no shared probe, because the shell either runs that loopback server or reports none. The reserved `openchamber:this-instance` entry still names the instance on screen, so the two never describe the same server. The row label is the switcher's own key (`desktopHostSwitcher.instance.local`), so both surfaces name that server the same way in every locale.
+- A jump to the app's own server goes through `activateLocalHost`, not the saved-host lookup, which does not hold Local. The endpoint switches in place on the app's own pages and the clicked session opens; the shell's reload branch is the fallback for a page that cannot switch in place.
+- A jump to another instance holds the clicked session for the target runtime key before switching (`sync/pending-session-open.ts`) and opens it only after the switch landed on that key, the new instance has its project list, and the session has been read by id. An activation that did not switch (a relay restore that stopped: no shell, host gone, already active) or that left another runtime active is not a jump: the hold is released and the row stays. A session the new instance cannot open (gone, or its folder not added) shows a toast instead of leaving the user on another session.
+- A poll names no directory, so it never starts an OpenCode location or an MCP server. It uses the saved host's own address, client token and request headers.
+- Reachability is the shared answer (`lib/desktopHostStatus.ts` `probeHost`), not a second probe. A relay answer hands its tunnel to the read, which closes it afterwards; a blocked status (`unreachable`, `wrong-service`, `incompatible`, `relay-limit`) reads nothing.
+- Relay identity comes from `descriptorsEqual` in `lib/relay/runtime-tunnel.ts`, so the switcher's relay and the poller's relay cannot disagree.
+- A `busy`/`retry` entry older than `HOST_STATUS_SEED_MAX_AGE_MS` is not current activity. An open turn suppresses unread for the session and every ancestor, matching the sidebar's `collectOpenTurnSessionIds`.
+- Unread reuses `sync/session-unread.ts`; a child's pending request attributes to its top-level parent, and archived sessions never ask.
+- A failed poll keeps the host's last known items and marks it unreachable; a partial answer keeps the half that answered. Offline reads nothing and waits at the retry cap. A failed host backs off, and a hidden window schedules no further cycle.
+- Removing a saved instance drops its rows; a tap on an unreachable host, or one no longer saved, keeps the surface open instead of switching blind.
+- The runtime gate is deliberate: desktop and mobile run the pollers, while web and VS Code do not show cross-instance attention because neither owns a saved-instance switcher.
+
+### Why a jump holds the session across the switch and not selects it at click time
+
+The click runs on the old instance; right after the switch the new one has neither its project list nor its session list, so a session in a folder it does not know yet could not be selected and its deferred open was cancelled by whatever selected next: the switch's own restore of that runtime's last session, the launch restore, the settings bootstrap adopting the saved active project, or the sidebar reacting to that project. The user landed on the previous session or a draft. The held open opens through the same opener a `?session=` route uses, after the bootstrap settings sync, and those automatic writers stand aside while it is held; any navigation by the user drops it.
diff --git a/packages/ui/src/lib/attentionDirectories.test.ts b/packages/ui/src/lib/attentionDirectories.test.ts
